\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Integrable observables and Gaussian expectation}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For the [Gaussian probability law](fdip-0005) #{\mu_Q} on
#{(E,h)}, an \newvocab{integrable observable} is a Borel function
#{F:E\to\mathbb R} with #{\int_E|F|\,d\mu_Q<\infty}.
Its \newvocab{Gaussian expectation} is
##{\begin{aligned}
\omega_Q(F)=\langle F\rangle_Q&=\int_E F\,d\mu_Q\\
 &=Z_Q^{-1}\int_E F(x)w_Q(x)\,d\lambda_E(x).
\end{aligned}}
The equality follows first for nonnegative simple functions from the
density definition, then by monotone convergence, and finally by
positive and negative parts for an absolutely integrable function.}
\p{These actual functions form a real vector space: the pointwise
bound #{|aF+bG|\leq|a||F|+|b||G|} proves closure, and integration
is real linear on it. One may instead take almost-everywhere classes
when using #{L^1}; polynomial observables here remain actual functions.
[All polynomial moments](fdip-002W) give its polynomial subalgebra;
the [state proposition](fdip-002X) proves normalization, positivity
and Hermiticity there. The [integrable-domain example](fdip-0030)
explains why the full vector space need not be an algebra.}
\p{This extends finite \vocabk{expectation}{ftip-000A}: absolute
integrability replaces automatic convergence of a finite sum.
The real-spin convention is
\citet{Definition 8.1 and Equation (8.3), p. 381}{friedli2017statistical};
the density-integral extension uses
\citet{Theorem 1.4.44, pp. 107-108, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
[Constants and oddness](fdip-002Y) give the vanishing linear means.
[Covariance and variance](fdip-002Z) extend the finite convention of
\ref{ftip-008H}; the partition function, two-point expectation and
other observable expectations remain distinct quantities.}
